<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bayat tribe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bayat are a Turkic tribe of the Oghuz confederation, listed among its tribes by Mahmud al-Kashgari in the 11th century. Bayat groups moved west with the Seljuk and later migrations into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> they were one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> tribes. Bayat khans ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maku">Maku</a> in north-western Iran from the 18th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bayat-tribe.jpg" alt="Bayat tribe" width="320" height="235" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ruzgar · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayat.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
